comment_text:
@errordeveloper re: passing config around, I expect this API to grow some extra legs at some point, but those can come as followups.

@jainvipin "but not a corresponding Uninit()" - Init() is mostly about initializing the in-memory plugin object.  Do you see need to use that same hook to do something deeper?  The problem with Uninit() is that it assumes it will always get called, but that isn't always the case (e.g. a crash).

@jainvipin "adopt name/value pairs as string-pair arg" the problem with adding more stuff to the CLI interface is that every exec-based plugin has to accept the full set of args, even if they don't need it.  Additionally, you have an API versioining problem - what API version are we sending to the plugins?  I'd rather provide very mininal args support and let plugins call back for more information.  There should be CLI tools that can make (versioned) API calls against kubelet for cases where plugins need more info.

@jainvipin "Do we need to catch the ‘die’ event on the pod, and pass it to the plugin" that sounds plausible, though we should first do everything we can to make resources self-cleaning.  For example, there's always the case of a pathological sequence like 1) run a pod P, 2) kubelet crashes, 3) pod P dies, 4) API server deletes P, 5) kubelet restarts.  You can see such pathology in cases where machines are thrashing - it sucks but we want to be correct in as many cases as we can.  Here what happens is that kubelet resumes, does not find P in the API server, does not find P running, and so does not do any cleanup.  If your net plugin would leak in this case, you should probably do some sort of init-time orphan cleanup (which is exactly what volume plugins do).  I filed a kubelet bug on this too.

@jainvipin "say I fixed a bug in my plugin" That sounds like a reasonable case, maybe a Sync() method that is called for each already-running pod when kubelet restarts?  Let's add something like that AFTER this goes in.  TBD: What happens if a pod is already running but was started without the net plugin?  e.g I started pod P, then I installed a net plugin and restarted kubelet.  Do we kill P?  Do we call sync() on it?  Do we leave it alone?  There are enough design options here that we can tackle that as phase 2.  Maybe part of pod status needs to be the net plugin name (and version?) that was used.

@jainvipin "while I found it out from the code, others may need to look for some documentation" yes, once this stabilizes a bit we need a solid doc on it.  Right now it is pre-alpha - everyone using it had better be looking at code.

@errordeveloper "Ideally, plugins shouldn't need to look up anything" - what API version should I pass to plugins?  That alone says to me that plugins probably have to get info rather than be pushed info.

@errordeveloper re: 5644 - kubelet should also run every pod in its own goroutines so no errors can block other pods.  But maybe a timeout would be good.  Not easy to implement reliably or with clean semantics.  and how long is long enough.  and should we give up after some number of timeouts, or keep trying?  Start simple.

re: IPAM - let's see how far we can go with this before we design another plugin system.  I agree that it should be codified if it is commonly used.

re: returning information - let's get this in and then tackle that.  I think we should capture stdout and stderr and log it at least.

