I see us doing a lot of function pointers lately, and it seems mostly to be a way to dodge adding a typedef.  I am not the deepest in Go, but it seems that it would be more Goan (Goish?  Goy?) to do

```
package framework
type Config interface {
    GetQueue() whatever
    GetListerWatcher() whatever
    ...
    Process()
}

type StaticConfig struct {
    Queue 
    Listerwatcher
   ...
}

func (sc *StaticConfig) GetQueue() { return sc.Queue }
```

and

```
type myConfig struct {
    framework.StaticConfig
}

func (mc *myConfig) Process() { ...}
```

Upon typing that out, I realize how tedious it is, but I want to raise the point anyway.  There's an inflection point somewhere, whereupon adding function pointers is less readable that this mess..

